//! # Structured concurrency
//!
//! `scope` owns child tasks. A `fork` starts work, and scope exit waits for all
//! children. `await task` joins a named value-producing fork. If one child
//! traps, sibling structured tasks are cancelled and the trap escapes the
//! scope; lambda actors are independent and are not sibling-cancelled.
//!
//! ```hew
//! fn double(value: i64) -> i64 { value * 2 }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     scope {
//!         fork left = double(10);
//!         fork right = double(11);
//!         let a = await left;
//!         let b = await right;
//!         println(f"{a},{b}");
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `join` is the actor-ask fan-out form. It issues every branch concurrently,
//! waits for all replies, and binds a tuple in source order. Two branches have
//! result shape `(R0, R1)`, not a list, map, or nested `Result`:
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Doubler {
//!     receive fn compute(value: i64) -> i64 { value * 2 }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let a = spawn Doubler;
//!     let b = spawn Doubler;
//!     let (first, second) = join {
//!         a.compute(10),
//!         b.compute(11),
//!     };
//!     assert_eq(first, 20);
//!     assert_eq(second, 22);
//!     println(f"{first},{second}");
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `scope` and `join` are distinct: scope manages task lifetime, while join
//! collects ordered actor replies. Cancellation is cooperative at suspension
//! points and is part of normal scope teardown, not an exception to the model.
